          "en": "Supreme People's Procuratorate of the People's Republic of China\nAnnouncement\nThe Reply of the Supreme People's Procuratorate on Whether an Infringer Who Obstructs, by Violence or Threat, a Person on the Staffing Establishment of a Public Institution in the Lawful Performance of Administrative Law Enforcement Duties May Be Treated as Committing the Crime of Obstructing Official Duties was adopted at the 58th meeting of the Ninth Procuratorial Committee of the Supreme People's Procuratorate on 21 March 2000, is hereby promulgated, and shall come into force on 24 April 2000.\nSupreme People's Procuratorate\n24 April 2000\nReply of the Supreme People's Procuratorate on Whether an Infringer Who Obstructs, by Violence or Threat,\na Person on the Staffing Establishment of a Public Institution in the Lawful Performance of Administrative\nLaw Enforcement Duties May Be Treated as Committing the Crime of Obstructing Official Duties\n(Adopted at the 58th meeting of the Ninth Procuratorial Committee\nof the Supreme People's Procuratorate on 21 March 2000)\nGao Jian Fa Shi Zi No. 2 [2000]\nChongqing Municipal People's Procuratorate:\nYour Procuratorate's Request for Instructions on Whether the Crime of Obstructing Official Duties May Be Applied to an Infringer Who Obstructs, by Violence or Threat, the Administrative Law Enforcement Activities of a Person on the Staffing Establishment of a Public Institution has been received. After study, the following reply is given:\nWhere a person obstructs, by violence or threat, a staff member of a State-owned public institution in the performance of administrative law enforcement duties in accordance with the provisions of laws and administrative regulations, or obstructs, by violence or threat, a person on the staffing establishment of a public institution who is entrusted by a State organ to engage in administrative law enforcement activities in the performance of administrative law enforcement duties, criminal responsibility may be investigated against the infringer for the crime of obstructing official duties.\nThis is our reply.\nSupreme People's Procuratorate\n24 April 2000"
